+    <section class="journal" aria-labelledby="journal-title">
+      <p class="journal-kicker">From origin to your morning ritual</p>
+      <h2 id="journal-title">Roaster's Journal</h2>
+      <article class="journal-copy" id="journal-copy" aria-label="Our bean sourcing process">
+        <p>A memorable cup begins long before the roaster warms up. Our sourcing process starts with the people who grow the coffee: producers, family farms, and cooperatives whose knowledge of their land shapes every harvest. We look for partners who can tell us where a lot was grown, how its cherries were picked, and how the coffee was processed. Knowing that story helps us choose beans with both character and a clear path back to their origin.</p>
+        <p>We follow the harvest rather than expecting every region to taste the same all year. As fresh lots become available, we request samples and taste them side by side in a process called cupping. We assess sweetness, acidity, body, and balance, but we also ask how those qualities will translate to your everyday brew. A bright, fruit-forward coffee might shine as a filter offering, while a rounder lot with chocolate notes could become the foundation of our espresso. We taste more than once before making a selection.</p>
+        <p>Quality in the cup is only one part of the decision. In conversations with producers and importing partners, we ask about pricing, working conditions, water use, and care for the soil. We value relationships that make room for honest answers and steady improvement. When a harvest impresses us, our goal is to return to that producer, share feedback, and build a relationship over time. We do not treat a single label as a substitute for understanding how a coffee was grown and traded.</p>
+        <p>After selection, careful handling protects the work already invested at origin. We track each lot through arrival, keep green beans in a cool, dry environment, and check samples for changes in condition. Our roasting team then develops a profile for that particular coffee, adjusting heat and time to bring out its natural sweetness without covering up its distinctive flavors. Small trial batches and repeated tastings guide those decisions before the beans reach our shelves.</p>
+        <p>The process continues in the café. We taste production batches, fine-tune our brewing recipes, and listen to what you notice in the cup. Seasonal changes are part of the pleasure: a new harvest can reveal a different side of a familiar origin. By staying curious from farm to final brew, we aim to serve coffee that honors the people behind it and gives you a reason to slow down, take a sip, and enjoy the details.</p>
+      </article>
+      <button class="btn" id="summarize-journal" type="button" aria-describedby="journal-status" aria-controls="journal-summary" disabled>Summarize</button>
+      <p class="journal-status" id="journal-status" role="status">Checking whether your browser supports local AI summaries…</p>
+      <noscript><p>Enable JavaScript to use the browser's local AI summary feature. The full journal is available above.</p></noscript>
+      <div id="journal-summary-region" aria-live="polite" aria-atomic="true">
+        <section class="journal-summary" id="journal-summary" aria-labelledby="journal-summary-title" hidden>
+          <h3 id="journal-summary-title">Sourcing at a glance</h3>
+          <ul id="journal-summary-points"></ul>
+        </section>
+      </div>
+    </section>
